    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d627-a3">

                <pb facs="1019" n="883" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg883"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3 in No.
                    155—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Thompson to Mr. St. Victor.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Portau Prince Hayti</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-11-23"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 23,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 68.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Minister</hi>: I have the honor of acknowledging
                    receipt of yours of this date, and in response thereto beg most respectfully to
                    remark that in the article speaking of the interest to be paid December 20
                    proximo, it would be well to note the amount as being 10,608 gourdes, instead of
                    10,000, “<hi rendition="#italic">sauf verification.</hi>”</p>
                <p>In the second case we are in accord that the interest from October 15 ultimo to
                    December 31 proximo, amounting to 2,210 piastres, be converted into the same
                    bonds as in the third and last case, viz, that the new bonds will be made out
                    the same in face value as the “<hi rendition="#italic">anciens titres</hi>”
                    which call for “<hi rendition="#italic">cent piastres fortes.</hi>”</p>
                <p>Expressing to you, Mr. Minister, the assurance of my most distinguished
                    consideration, I allow myself to remark that in such equitable manner it is
                    certain the representatives of our respective countries may always be enabled to
                    conserve the most cordial relations between the two Governments.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John E. W.
                    Thompson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
